## 14. Reporting problems

Report to [fill in: channel, e.g. the deviation / nonconformity process] without delay when:

- an AI-assisted change that passed review turns out to be defective;
- a guardrail did not behave as expected (a denied action ran, a gate did not block, a sandbox or hook failed);
- the tool used or offered a model outside the envelope, or switched model unexpectedly;
- you find an AI tool, bot, extension or agent that is not in section 4;
- you suspect that secrets, health data or personal data reached an AI tool;
- you notice an attempted prompt injection.

## 15. Training and competence

- Users and reviewers complete training on this work instruction before they get access or approve their first AI-assisted change{{#reg_iso13485}} (ISO 13485 clause 6.2){{/reg_iso13485}}. Training covers: these rules; typical AI failure modes (plausible but wrong code, invented dependencies, missed defects, prompt injection, automation bias); the review checklist; data rules; reporting.
- Training is repeated when this work instruction changes materially, or when a new tool, surface or context of use is introduced.
- Training records are kept per [fill in: training procedure].
- Effectiveness of the training is checked by (required{{#reg_iso13485}}, ISO 13485 clause 6.2 c){{/reg_iso13485}}): [fill in, e.g. the reviewer-effectiveness sample PQ-02 and periodic review findings]. The method is proportionate to the risk of the work.
- Where the EU AI Act applies, this training is part of the measures supporting AI literacy (AI Act Article 4).

